(defmodule language-core
  "The machinery of the non-Lisp language modes. `deflanguage` declares a tree-sitter language
from ONE spec (faces, indent, heredocs, formatter) and generates its whole service set — Ruby, Elixir,
Python, Rust, the brace languages, C#, CSS, HTML, go.mod, HCL / Terraform, shell, JSON, TOML,
YAML and Dockerfile are its clients, each a face table and an indent spec; the last five
keep their line lexer as the stand-in for a missing grammar (`:fallback`). `deflexer` is its
sibling for the fontify-only layers over the `lex-*` modules. The languages themselves are the
`lang-*` family files; a macro here names the module it expands in (`reflect/current-ns`).")
;; editor/treesit, editor/highlight and the std lexers are referenced qualified below —
;; each `mod/name` auto-loads its module.

(defmacro deflanguage (name spec)
  "Declare a tree-sitter language mode from ONE spec, and register it.

    (deflanguage elixir
      {:lang :elixir
       :pattern \"[.](ex|exs)$\"
       :faces elixir-face-table
       :indent elixir-indent-spec
       :heredocs *triple-quote-heredocs*
       :comment \"# \"})

A language supplies DATA and gets the whole service set — a `-fontify`, a restart, an
`-indent`, a layer naming them, and the registration — or fails at load saying which part
is missing. Written out by hand per language, a mode is the place a service could be
forgotten, and a forgotten service is invisible until someone presses the key.

The services are generated as named globals rather than closures because
`model/ed-mode-service` resolves a service SYMBOL late (`reflect/eval`) — which is what
lets a redefined fontify take effect on the next frame without reloading the mode. A
closure in the layer would be resolved once and frozen.

`:format` is the language's formatter as a command vector — `[\"mix\" \"format\"]`, to which
the file path is appended — or a vector of them, `[[\"terraform\" \"fmt\"] [\"tofu\" \"fmt\"]]`,
when several programs format the language: the first installed one runs
(`formatter/format-command-choice`). It becomes the `:format-command` service that `M-x
format-buffer` and format-on-save consult, so neither names a language.

`:definitions` says which nodes C-M-a / C-M-e / C-M-h treat as a definition —
`{:kinds #{:function_item …}}`, or `:calls` for a language that defines with a call
(`editor/treesit/register-definitions`). Ruby and Elixir are declared by std, whose
grammars they are; a language that declares nothing moves by top-level form.

`:grammar` is where the language's grammar lives and WHICH revision — `{:url … :revision …}`,
with `:subdir` for a repository holding several — so `M-x grammar-install` can fetch and build
exactly that one, and opening a file of the language without one says so
(`grammars/declare-grammar`). The revision is the commit the spec's tables were written
against: a grammar's repository moves on its own, and a later revision may rename a node the
face table names. The names the tables use are registered too (`expect-grammar-names`), so an
installed grammar of another revision that lacks one is reported and stood in for rather than
used (`grammarcheck`, brood ADR-583). The pins are the commits each installed grammar was built
from when the specs were last checked line by line (each rebuilt byte-identical to the library
in use); moving one is a deliberate edit, made with the specs' tests passing against it.
`:fingerprint` is that revision's SHAPE (`editor/treesit/grammar-fingerprint`: its ABI, node
kinds and fields), so a library built by hand, with no install manifest to say its revision,
is still known to be the pinned one when its shape matches — and known to differ when not.

`:type` is the buffer type the mode registers as, when it is not the grammar's name: one
grammar may serve two modes that differ in something else — HCL's parses Terraform and
plain HCL, and only Terraform's files format with `terraform fmt`. Without it the type is
`:lang`, and a second mode over the same grammar would take the first one's files.

`:indent-tabs` true writes indentation as tabs (Go's gofmt'd sources), at the indenter's
column. With `:indent-width`, `:tab-width` and `:fill-column` it is the language's
CONVENTION (`conventions`): the indent width is the `:indent` spec's `:width` unless
`:indent-width` says otherwise (`:tab` — one level is one tab, Go's), and a buffer's
`.editorconfig` or the user's entry for the mode overrides any of them. The indenter counts
a level as the buffer's width, not the spec's.

`:fallback` names a line lexer by its `deflexer` stem (`'lex-shell/shell`) for a language
that had one before it had a grammar. The grammar is a separate download, and a parsed mode
without it colours nothing; with a fallback, a buffer opened while the grammar is missing is
typed as the lexer's mode instead (`model/ed-grammar-fallback`), and becomes the parsed mode
when `M-x grammar-install` or `grammar-reload` brings the grammar (`model/ed-grammars-arrived`).
`:fallback-above` is a size in characters past which the lexer serves even with the grammar —
JSON's, where a held tree of a large machine-written file costs more than it buys.

`:injections` is the code EMBEDDED in the language (brood ADR-489): a vector of injection
rules, `[{:content {:kind :template_literal :parent :heredoc_template} :lang-from
:heredoc_identifier :aliases {\"BASH\" :bash}}]` — which nodes hold another language's text
and how its language is named. The embedded language's faces are its own `deflanguage`'s,
found when a frame asks (`spec-injections`); one with no spec or no grammar keeps this
language's colours. Checked at registration like the face table.

`:interpreters` lists the `#!` program names that select the mode for a file with no telling
name, as `deflexer`'s does.

`:fontify-restart` says where a windowed fontify may cut its lead-in, and comes from one of
three declarations. `:heredocs` (paired markers, Elixir's `\"\"\"`) restarts at a column-0 line
outside them (`editor/treesit/column0-restart`). `:restart {:item-start :opaque}` is the
brace languages' rule (`column0-item-restart`): the column-0 line that LOOKS like an item
start, unless something whose contents can be anything (a block comment, a raw string)
opens above it. `:restart 'sym` names a hand-written one when the rule is neither — Ruby
must also notice `=begin`. A language with none declines to restart, which is a fact about
the language — `editor/treesit/language-contract` says which services are required and
which are optional.

The generated restart is `<name>-restart`, not `<name>-fontify-restart`: the obvious name is
the one a language with a CUSTOM restart uses (Ruby's), and generating it would silently
replace that one. A generated definition must not be able to take a hand-written name.

The spec is evaluated ONCE, into `<name>-spec`, so a spec built from a shared base
(`(merge js-spec {…})`) is one value every generated service reads."
  (let (base (->string name)
        generated (fn (suffix) (symbol (str base suffix)))
        qualified (fn (suffix) (symbol (str (reflect/current-ns) "/" base suffix)))
        spec-name (generated "-spec")
        spec-ref (qualified "-spec"))
    `(do
       (def ~spec-name ~spec)
       (defn ~(generated "-fontify") (text) (language-core/spec-fontify ~spec-ref text))
       ;; the embedded languages' injection spec, built per call: an injected language's
       ;; faces are ITS spec's, and it may be registered after this one
       (defn ~(generated "-ts-injections") () (language-core/spec-injections ~spec-ref))
       ;; `tree`: the buffer's held tree for exactly `text`, when the caller has one
       ;; (`indent/ed-indent-target`) — the indenter walks it instead of parsing `text`, which
       ;; is then the buffer's rope: only the lines it inspects are read (brood ADR-483)
       (defn ~(generated "-indent") (text line-start &optional (tree nil))
         (language-core/spec-indent-column ~spec-ref text line-start tree))
       (defn ~(generated "-restart") (source position)
         (language-core/spec-restart ~spec-ref source position))
       ;; show-paren over the parse: the view's window of text, or the held tree
       ;; (`:bracket-match-tree`, `tsbrackets/match-tree`) when the buffer keeps one
       (defn ~(generated "-bracket-match") (text position)
         (tsbrackets/match-text text (get ~spec-ref :lang) position))
       ;; what a fold at a line covers: the block the parse opens there (`codefold`)
       (defn ~(generated "-fold-extent") (rope line)
         (when (language-core/spec-grammar? ~spec-ref)
           (codefold/tree-fold-extent (get ~spec-ref :lang)
             (language-core/spec-fold-kinds ~spec-ref)
             rope
             line)))
       ;; the definitions point is inside, for the header line (`codecrumbs`)
       (defn ~(generated "-breadcrumbs") (buffer &optional (tree nil))
         (codecrumbs/tree-crumbs (get ~spec-ref :lang) buffer tree))
       ;; the face table as a global the `:ts-faces` service names, so a held tree is
       ;; coloured with the table the fontify uses (`editor/treesit/fontify-tree`)
       (def ~(generated "-ts-faces") (get ~spec-ref :faces))
       (def ~(generated "-mode-layer")
         (language-core/language-layer '~name
           ~spec-ref
           {:fontify '~(qualified "-fontify")
            :indent '~(qualified "-indent")
            :restart '~(qualified "-restart")
            :faces '~(qualified "-ts-faces")
            :injections '~(qualified "-ts-injections")
            :bracket-match '~(qualified "-bracket-match")
            :fold-extent '~(qualified "-fold-extent")
            :breadcrumbs '~(qualified "-breadcrumbs")}))
       ;; the line-lexer stand-in a buffer is typed as while the grammar is missing; nil
       ;; for a language with no `:fallback`
       (def ~(generated "-fallback-layer") (language-core/fallback-layer '~name ~spec-ref))
       (language-core/declare-definitions ~spec-ref)
       (language-core/register-spec ~spec-ref '~spec-ref)
       (language-core/declare-grammar ~spec-ref)
       (language-core/expect-grammar-names ~spec-ref)
       (language-core/register-fallback ~spec-ref '~(qualified "-fallback-layer"))
       (doseq (program (get ~spec-ref :interpreters []))
         (modes/register-interpreter program (language-core/spec-type ~spec-ref)))
       (modes/register-prog-mode '~(qualified "-mode-layer") (language-core/spec-type ~spec-ref)))))

(defn spec-indent-width (spec)
  "The indent width a `deflanguage` or `deflexer` spec declares as its language's convention:
its `:indent-width` (a number, or `:tab` for one tab), else its indent spec's `:width`, else
nil — a language with no say, which takes the user's default.

    (language-core/spec-indent-width {:indent {:width 4}})   ;=> 4
    (language-core/spec-indent-width {:indent-width :tab :indent {:width 8}})   ;=> :tab"
  (or (get spec :indent-width)
    (let (indent (get spec :indent)) (when (map? indent) (get indent :width)))))

(defn spec-indent (indent)
  "The indent spec a generated `deflanguage` indenter walks with: `indent` with its `:width`
the buffer's indent width when the caller says one (`conventions/bound-indent-width`, bound by
`indent/ed-indent-target`), else as declared."
  (let (width (conventions/bound-indent-width))
    (if (and (map? indent) (int? width)) (assoc indent :width width) indent)))

;; A language with no line-lexer stand-in stays its parsed mode without its grammar (not
;; installed, failed to load, quarantined — `model/ed-grammar-fallback`), so its services are
;; asked with no grammar to parse by: the view fontifies every frame, and a parse that raised
;; painted `render error` over the whole window, every frame; RET indents. Without one the file
;; shows uncoloured (the visit's note names M-x grammar-install), RET keeps the line's
;; indentation and nothing folds.

(defn spec-grammar? (spec)
  "Whether the runtime has a grammar for a `deflanguage` spec's language to parse by."
  (model/grammar-loadable? (get spec :lang)))

(defn spec-fontify (spec text)
  "The `[start end face]` spans a `deflanguage` spec's grammar colours `text` with — none
without a grammar ([[spec-grammar?]])."
  (if (spec-grammar? spec)
    (editor/treesit/fontify text (get spec :lang) (get spec :faces) true (spec-injections spec))
    []))

(defn spec-indent-column (spec text line-start tree)
  "The column a `deflanguage` spec indents the line at `line-start` of `text` to: from the
parse (`tree` when the caller holds one), its content's own for `:indent :content`
([[content-indent]]), and the same — the line's own, the previous line's for a blank one —
without a grammar ([[spec-grammar?]])."
  (let (indent (get spec :indent))
    (if (or (= indent :content) (and (nil? tree) (not (spec-grammar? spec))))
      (content-indent text line-start)
      (editor/treesit/indent-column text (get spec :lang) line-start (spec-indent indent) tree))))

(defn spec-type (spec)
  "The buffer type a `deflanguage` spec registers as: its `:type`, else its `:lang`."
  (or (get spec :type) (get spec :lang)))

(defn fallback-type (spec)
  "The buffer type of a `deflanguage`'s line-lexer stand-in — `:shell-lexer` for shell — or
nil for a language with no `:fallback`."
  (when (get spec :fallback) (keyword (str (->string (spec-type spec)) "-lexer"))))

(defn fallback-layer (name spec)
  "The layer a `deflanguage` with a `:fallback` lexer stem stands in with while its grammar
is missing (`model/ed-grammar-fallback`): its line-lexer mode, under the same name, and
saying which grammar it wants (`:wants-grammar`) so `M-x grammar-install` offers it first.
It keeps the language's formatter: `shfmt` formats a script whether or not it was parsed.
Nil without a `:fallback`."
  (when (get spec :fallback)
    (assoc (lexer-layer name
             {:pattern (get spec :pattern)
              :lexer (get spec :fallback)
              :comment (get spec :comment)
              ;; the language's conventions are the stand-in's too
              :indent-tabs (get spec :indent-tabs)
              :indent-width (spec-indent-width spec)
              :tab-width (get spec :tab-width)
              :fill-column (get spec :fill-column)
              :pairs (get spec :pairs)
              :strings (get spec :strings)})
      :wants-grammar (get spec :lang)
      :format-command (get spec :format))))

(defn register-fallback (spec layer-ref)
  "Bind a `deflanguage`'s stand-in type to its fallback layer over text-mode. No file pattern
names the type: a buffer reaches it only as the stand-in for the parsed mode."
  (let (type (fallback-type spec))
    (when type (modes/register-type type (list layer-ref 'modes/text-mode-layer)))))

(defn content-indent (text line-start)
  "The indentation a `deflanguage` with `:indent :content` gives the line starting at `line-start`
of `text` (a string or a rope): its OWN, so TAB changes nothing — and, for a blank line
(the RET case), the previous non-blank line's, so the content's indentation continues.
YAML's: its columns are its data, and no tree can say where a line belongs.

    (language-core/content-indent \"a:\\n    b: 1\\n\" 3)   ;=> 4
    (language-core/content-indent \"a:\\n  b: 1\\n\\n\" 10)   ;=> 2"
  (let (rope (if (string? text) (text/string-> text) text)
        ;; a COLUMN: the leading whitespace's display width at the ambient tab width
        own (fn (line)
              (string/display-width (string/substring line
                                      0
                                      (- (count line)
                                        (count (string/trim-left line))))))
        blank? (fn (line) (= (string/trim line) ""))
        line-index (text/char->line rope line-start)
        line (text/line rope line-index))
    (if (not (blank? line))
      (own line)
      (letrec (back (fn (above)
                      (cond
                        (< above 0) 0
                        (blank? (text/line rope above)) (back (dec above))
                        else (own (text/line rope above)))))
        (back (dec line-index))))))

(defn spec-restart (spec source position)
  "The `:fontify-restart` a `deflanguage` spec declares as data: a `:restart` map's item rule
(`column0-item-restart`), else `:heredocs`' paired-marker rule, else `position` itself — which is
only reached for a language whose layer names no generated restart."
  (let (rule (get spec :restart)
        heredocs (get spec :heredocs))
    (cond
      (map? rule) (column0-item-restart source position (:item-start rule) (:opaque rule))
      (not (nil? heredocs)) (editor/treesit/column0-restart source position heredocs)
      else position)))

(defn declare-definitions (spec)
  "Register a `deflanguage` spec's `:definitions` with `editor/treesit`. A spec without one
leaves std's declaration alone — Ruby's and Elixir's live there."
  (let (definitions (get spec :definitions))
    (when (map? definitions)
      (editor/treesit/register-definitions (get spec :lang) definitions))))

(defn declare-grammar (spec)
  "Record where a `deflanguage` spec's grammar lives (`:grammar` — a git URL, or `{:url
:subdir}` for a repository holding several), so `M-x grammar-install` can fetch it and a
file of the language opened without one can say how to get it."
  (let (grammar (get spec :grammar))
    (when (not (nil? grammar))
      (grammars/declare-grammar (get spec :lang) grammar))))

(defn spec-grammar-names (spec)
  "The node kinds and fields of its grammar a `deflanguage` spec's tables name, as
`{:kinds :fields}` (std's `editor/treesit/language-names`): its face table, injection rules,
indent spec and definitions.

    (language-core/spec-grammar-names {:faces {:comment :syntax/comment} :indent :content})   ;=> {:kinds #{:comment} :fields #{}}"
  (editor/treesit/language-names {:faces (get spec :faces)
                                  :injections (get spec :injections)
                                  :indent (get spec :indent)
                                  :definitions (get spec :definitions)}))

(defn expect-grammar-names (spec)
  "Record the names a `deflanguage` spec's tables use of its grammar (`spec-grammar-names`),
so the grammar installed is checked for them before it is used (`grammarcheck`, brood
ADR-583) — under the spec's buffer type, since one grammar may serve two modes."
  (grammarcheck/expect-names (get spec :lang) (spec-type spec) (spec-grammar-names spec)))

;; ---- injections: another language's code inside this one (brood ADR-489) ---------------
;; A spec's `:injections` is the RULES of an ADR-489 injection spec: which nodes hold which
;; language's text. The faces are not written there. Each embedded language colours with the
;; table its own `deflanguage` declares, looked up when a frame asks, so the shell inside a
;; Terraform heredoc follows shell-mode's table and a language registered later is found.

(defn register-spec (spec spec-ref)
  "Record that `spec-ref` (the `<name>-spec` global's symbol) is the spec of the tree-sitter
language `(:lang spec)`, where `modes/language-faces` finds an embedded language's table. Stored
as the symbol and resolved per lookup, so a re-evaluated spec is the one read."
  (ownership/claim! :languages/specs (get spec :lang) spec-ref))

(defn- rule-languages (rule)
  ;; every language a rule can name: its fixed `:lang`, or its aliases' targets
  (if (get rule :lang) [(get rule :lang)] (vals (or (get rule :aliases) {}))))

(defn injection-spec (rules faces-of)
  "The injection spec `{:rules :faces}` for `rules`, each named language's table from
`(faces-of language)`. A language with no table is left out: a rule fixed to it is dropped, an
alias to it removed. Nil when no rule is left, which a fontify reads as no injections."
  (let (faces (fold (mapcat rules rule-languages)
                {}
                (fn (tables language)
                  (if (contains? tables language)
                    tables
                    (let (table (faces-of language))
                      (if table (assoc tables language table) tables)))))
        kept (seq/keep rules
               (fn (rule)
                 (cond
                   (get rule :lang) (when (contains? faces (get rule :lang)) rule)
                   (get rule :aliases)
                     (assoc rule
                       :aliases
                         (into {}
                           (seq/filter (get rule :aliases)
                             (fn ([_ language]) (contains? faces language)))))
                   else rule))))
    (when (not (empty? kept)) {:rules (vec kept) :faces faces})))

(defn spec-injections (spec)
  "The injection spec a `deflanguage` spec's `:injections` rules make with the registered
languages' tables (`injection-spec`), or nil for a language that embeds none."
  (let (rules (get spec :injections))
    (when (not (empty? rules)) (injection-spec rules modes/language-faces))))

(defn injection-rule-problems (rules)
  "What is malformed in a `deflanguage`'s `:injections` rules (`editor/treesit/injection-problems`),
each named language given an empty table: whether the language has a real one is a question
for the frame, when every language has registered."
  (let (placeholder (into {} (map (mapcat rules rule-languages) (fn (language) [language {}]))))
    (editor/treesit/injection-problems {:rules rules :faces placeholder})))

(defn spec-fold-kinds (spec)
  "The node kinds a fold in `spec`'s language covers: its indent spec's `{:blocks :brackets}`, the
nodes that open a level — nil for a language whose indent reads content (`:indent :content`).

    (language-core/spec-fold-kinds {:indent {:blocks #{:block} :brackets #{:list}}})   ;=> {:blocks #{:block} :brackets #{:list}}"
  (let (indent (get spec :indent))
    (when (map? indent) {:blocks (get indent :blocks #{}) :brackets (get indent :brackets #{})})))

(defn language-layer (name spec refs)
  "The mode layer for a `deflanguage`, checked against `editor/treesit/language-contract`.
`refs` names the generated services: `{:fontify :indent :restart :faces :injections
:bracket-match}`.

Raises on an incomplete language rather than registering one. A mode missing `:indent`
would register happily and simply do nothing on RET — a failure that surfaces as a key
which feels broken, in one language, much later. A malformed face table is refused the
same way (`editor/treesit/face-table-problems`): the kernel would refuse it too, but at
the first frame drawn, and a misspelt selector key (`:feild`) would otherwise be found
by someone opening a file."
  (let (rule (get spec :restart)
        layer {:name name
               :ts-lang (get spec :lang)
               ;; the `{selector :syntax/face}` table, for colouring a HELD tree
               ;; (`syntaxtree`, brood ADR-443): `:fontify` takes text, and a view that
               ;; keeps the document's parse asks the tree with the same table instead
               :ts-faces (:faces refs)
               ;; a fn answering the injection spec the held tree is coloured with — the
               ;; same one `:fontify` passes — for a language whose spec embeds others
               :ts-injections (when (get spec :injections) (:injections refs))
               :file-pattern (get spec :pattern)
               :fontify (:fontify refs)
               :indent (:indent refs)
               ;; a hand-written one by name (Ruby's, which must also see `=begin`), the
               ;; generated one for a rule declared as data, or none — a language with no
               ;; safe cut declines, which is a fact about it rather than a hole in the mode
               :fontify-restart (cond
                                  (symbol? rule) rule
                                  (or (map? rule) (get spec :heredocs)) (:restart refs)
                                  else nil)
               :comment-syntax (get spec :comment)
               ;; `[open close]` for a language whose only comment is a block (CSS, HTML),
               ;; which a line prefix cannot write
               :comment-block (get spec :comment-block)
               ;; the bracket pair at point, from the parse: a `(` in a string matches nothing
               :bracket-match (:bracket-match refs)
               ;; a fold covers the block the parse opens at its line (Emacs's hideshow)
               :fold-extent (:fold-extent refs)
               ;; the header line's path of definitions at point
               :breadcrumbs (:breadcrumbs refs)
               :bracket-match-tree 'tsbrackets/match-tree
               ;; The language's own formatter, as `[prog arg …]` with the file appended
               ;; — `mix format <file>`. A service like any other, so `M-x format-buffer`
               ;; and format-on-save ask the BUFFER what formats it and never name a
               ;; language. Absent for a language with no formatter, which is a fact
               ;; about the language.
               :format-command (get spec :format)
               ;; the words that end a block, as a regexp over what precedes point on its line
               ;; (`indent/ed-electric-reindent`): typing one reindents the line
               :electric-indent (get spec :electric-indent)
               ;; the line-lexer type a buffer stands in with while the grammar is missing,
               ;; or past `:fallback-above` characters (`model/ed-grammar-fallback`)
               :grammar-fallback (when (fallback-type spec)
                                   {:type (fallback-type spec) :above (get spec :fallback-above)})}
        layer (merge layer
                (spec-conventions spec)
                ;; Go's tab-indented sources: the indenter answers a column, and a mode that
                ;; indents with tabs writes it as tabs (`conventions/indentation`). False here
                ;; is a statement (Python is spaces), which a user's `:default` does not
                ;; override; their entry for the mode does
                {:indent-tabs (= true (get spec :indent-tabs))})
        gaps (editor/treesit/missing-services layer)
        face-problems (editor/treesit/face-table-problems (get spec :faces))
        injection-problems (injection-rule-problems (get spec :injections [])))
    (when (not (empty? gaps))
      (error "deflanguage " name ": missing required service(s) " (->string gaps)))
    (when (not (empty? face-problems))
      (error "deflanguage " name ": malformed face table: " (string/join face-problems "; ")))
    (when (not (empty? injection-problems))
      (error "deflanguage "
        name
        ": malformed injections: "
        (string/join injection-problems "; ")))
    layer))

;; ---- the line-lexer modes: env, JSON5 and the configuration formats -------------------
;; A language with no grammar here is lexed a line at a time by a pure module (`lex-dotenv`,
;; `lex-configs`; shell's and Dockerfile's serve as `:fallback`s): its buffers keep every
;; text-mode binding and gain colouring. The `:file-pattern` is matched against the file's
;; basename, so a bare `.env` or `Makefile` triggers. Each restarts at the viewport's own line
;; (`editor/highlight/line-restart`: no cross-line state worth a backscan) and keeps each
;; line's spans as a memo (`:line-lexer`, brood ADR-471).

(defmacro deflexer (name spec)
  "Declare a line-lexer language mode from ONE spec, and register it — the sibling of
`deflanguage` for a language lexed line by line rather than parsed.

    (deflexer yaml
      {:pattern \"[.]ya?ml$\"
       :lexer 'lex-configs/yaml
       :comment \"# \"})

`:lexer` names the lexer by its stem: the module defines `<stem>-spans`, the whole-text
`:fontify`, and `<stem>-line-lexer`, the `:line-lexer` the pane's line cache threads.
`:comment` is the comment token `M-;` writes. `:interpreters` lists the `#!` program names
that select the mode for a file with no telling name (`modes/register-interpreter`).

A spec's keys are the language's facts; the layer's are the editor's (`:comment` becomes
`:comment-syntax`, which every reader asks for), and only this maps one to the other."
  (let (base (->string name)
        layer (symbol (str base "-mode-layer"))
        layer-ref (symbol (str (reflect/current-ns) "/" base "-mode-layer")))
    `(do
       (def ~layer (language-core/lexer-layer '~name ~spec))
       (doseq (program (get ~spec :interpreters []))
         (modes/register-interpreter program (keyword '~name)))
       (modes/register-language-mode '~layer-ref (keyword '~name)))))

(defn lexer-layer (name spec)
  "The mode layer a `deflexer` spec declares: `:fontify` and `:line-lexer` from the `:lexer`
stem, the line restart, and `:comment-syntax`. Raises on a spec with no `:pattern` or
`:lexer`, which would register a mode no file reaches or one that colours nothing."
  (let ({:keys [pattern lexer comment]} spec)
    (when (not (and (string? pattern) (symbol? lexer)))
      (error "deflexer " name ": needs a :pattern string and a :lexer symbol: " (pr-str spec)))
    (merge
      {:name name
       :file-pattern pattern
       :fontify (symbol (str lexer "-spans"))
       :line-lexer (symbol (str lexer "-line-lexer"))
       :fontify-restart 'editor/highlight/line-restart
       :comment-syntax comment
       ;; nil — no say — unless the spec has one
       :indent-tabs (get spec :indent-tabs)
       ;; a config format is written like code — keys, names, values from the file — so
       ;; its completion card opens on its own, unless the spec is prose (a commit message)
       :completion-auto (get spec :completion-auto true)}
      (spec-conventions spec))))

(defn- spec-conventions (spec)
  "The layer keys both spec kinds take from a spec as they stand: the language's conventions
(`conventions` — each nil when the spec has no say), the electric pairs its strings and
brackets open (`electric`, nil for the default brackets and double quote), and its strings
and comments (`stringstate`, nil for its quote pairs as one-line strings)."
  {:indent-width (spec-indent-width spec)
   :tab-width (get spec :tab-width)
   :fill-column (get spec :fill-column)
   :electric-pairs (get spec :pairs)
   :string-syntax (get spec :strings)})

;; ---- face-table helpers ------------------------------------------------------------------

(defn token-faces (tokens face)
  "Face-table entries colouring each ANONYMOUS token in `tokens` (their text, as strings)
`face` — for a token the keyword rule cannot see because it does not begin with a letter,
as C's `#include` and CSS's `@media`.

    (get (language-core/token-faces [\"#if\"] :syntax/keyword) (keyword \"#if\"))   ;=> :syntax/keyword"
  (into {} (map tokens (fn (token) [(keyword token) face]))))

;; ---- electric pairs: what each language's strings open ----------------------------------
;; A spec's `:pairs` is its `electric` pairs, the syntax table's string delimiters in Emacs's
;; terms. Without one a language has `electric/*default-pairs*` — the brackets and `"` — which
;; is right where `'` is not a string (Rust's lifetimes, JSON, HCL). A language whose `'` opens
;; a string pairs it; one whose backtick does (a template, a raw string, a command) pairs that.
(def *quote-pairs* [["(" ")"] ["[" "]"] ["{" "}"] ["\"" "\""] ["'" "'"]])
(def *backtick-pairs* (conj *quote-pairs* ["`" "`"]))
;; Python's triple quotes: `""` + `"` opens `"""|"""`
(def *python-pairs* (into *quote-pairs* [["\"\"\"" "\"\"\""] ["'''" "'''"]]))

;; A spec's `:strings` is its string and comment syntax (`stringstate`): which delimiters open a
;; string, which may span lines, which do not escape, and the comment tokens — what tells a
;; quote that opens a string from one that closes it, keeps a bracket inside a string out of the
;; balance, and finds the docstring M-q fills, all with no grammar. Without one a language's
;; quote pairs are one-line strings (`stringstate/pairs-syntax`), which is JSON's.
(def *hash-strings* {:strings [{:open "\""} {:open "'"}] :comments ["#"]})
(def *c-strings*
  {:strings [{:open "\""} {:open "'"}] :comments ["//"] :block-comments [["/*" "*/"]]})
(def *js-strings*
  (assoc *c-strings* :strings [{:open "\""} {:open "'"} {:open "`" :multiline true}]))
(def *go-strings*
  (assoc *c-strings* :strings [{:open "\""} {:open "'"} {:open "`" :multiline true :raw true}]))
(def *rust-strings*
  ;; `'x'` is a character, `'a` a lifetime: a quote is a char literal only when one closes it
  {:strings [{:open "\"" :multiline true}]
   :char-literal "'"
   :comments ["//"]
   :block-comments [["/*" "*/"]]})
(def *python-strings*
  {:strings [{:open "\"\"\"" :multiline true}
              {:open "'''" :multiline true}
              {:open "\""}
              {:open "'"}]
   :comments ["#"]})
(def *toml-strings*
  {:strings [{:open "\"\"\"" :multiline true}
              {:open "'''" :multiline true :raw true}
              {:open "\""}
              {:open "'" :raw true}]
   :comments ["#"]})
(def *ruby-strings*
  {:strings [{:open "\"" :multiline true} {:open "'" :multiline true} {:open "`" :multiline true}]
   :comments ["#"]})
(def *elixir-strings*
  {:strings [{:open "\"\"\"" :multiline true}
              {:open "'''" :multiline true}
              {:open "\"" :multiline true}
              {:open "'" :multiline true}]
   :comments ["#"]})
(def *shell-strings*
  {:strings [{:open "\"" :multiline true}
              {:open "'" :multiline true :raw true}
              {:open "`" :multiline true}]
   :comments ["#"]
   ;; `#` opens a comment only at a word's start: `${#x}` and `$#` are words
   :comment-word-start true})
(def *css-strings* {:strings [{:open "\""} {:open "'"}] :block-comments [["/*" "*/"]]})
(def *html-strings* {:strings [{:open "\""} {:open "'"}] :block-comments [["<!--" "-->"]]})
(def *hcl-strings* {:strings [{:open "\""}] :comments ["#" "//"] :block-comments [["/*" "*/"]]})

;; ---- the restarts more than one language shares ---------------------------------------------

;; Paired multi-line string markers — what the restart counts to decide whether a column-0
;; line is real code or prose inside one (Elixir's `@moduledoc`, Python's docstrings).
(def *triple-quote-heredocs* ["\"\"\"" "'''"])

(defn- item-line-before (source position item-start)
  ;; the nearest column-0 line at or before `position` that matches `item-start`, or 0
  (let (candidate (editor/treesit/column0-before source position)
        line-end (let (newline (index-of source "\n" candidate))
                   (if (< newline 0) (count source) newline)))
    (cond
      (= candidate 0) 0
      (regex/match? (string/substring source candidate line-end) item-start) candidate
      else (item-line-before source (dec candidate) item-start))))

(defn column0-item-restart (source position item-start opaque-opener)
  "The fontify restart every brace language shares: the previous column-0 line that
LOOKS like the start of an item (`item-start`, a regex over the line), unless something
whose contents can be anything — a block comment, a raw or template string
(`opaque-opener`) — opens above it in this window; then the whole lead-in. A language with
no such thing passes nil."
  (let (candidate (item-line-before source position item-start)
        opener (when opaque-opener (regex/find source opaque-opener)))
    (if (and opener (< (get opener :start) candidate)) 0 candidate)))
